Romhack Base: Twilight Princess
Timeline Placement: Not too long after Twilight Princess
Length: Middle (1 full Romhack)
Significance: Traumatic journey for Link; Significant lore implications

Synopsis:
Link is travelling through a wooded area on Epona at night. Branches are rustling in the wind. Link is in full hero gear. Epona is anxious.
Something is watching Link. It skims along the ground, approaching the hero rapidly, lighting everything before it in dull red light, light that is reflected in the hero's startled eyes-!
(Title Drop)

[Arc 1: Beginning]
Link awakens in bed back home. He's wearing his old Ordonian clothes. Sky outside is a dull orange. The spot outside in view of his window is empty. (No music plays.)
There are no birds outside. No squirrels either. Nor is Epona present. The gate to Faron Woods is shut. No signs of life at the Spirit Spring.
Ordon Village is without its people. Without its cuccoos and its cats and its hawk and its bees. There aren't even any ripe pumpkins.
The ranch seems likewise empty... at first. But the gate shuts behind Link, and Stallord's skull bursts out of the barn.
Link has no weapons, not that any of them would affect the thing anyway. The only thing he can do is run into the barn it came out of.
The thing barely follows Link, opting rather to shoot balls of fire down the goat aisle at him as he ducks into stalls to hide, hardly noticing the unnatural mess on the walls.
The barn extends deeper than it should. It gets darker as he goes. By the time Stallord stops shooting at him, the barn has disappeared altogether.
A tiny figure appears in the distance. It approaches.
It is Zant. He gets closer, closer, closer- he's still growing!
He stomps on Link-!
Link awakens chained to the wall of Hyrule Castle's dungeon.
A light passes over him. The chains fade, and Link easily steps forward. The cell door swings open silently.
Link escapes the cell and explores the castle, eventually making his way up to Zelda's room.
There, a different cloaked figure stands before the window, its back towards him.
It turns-

Link finds himself chained to a wooden crossbeam in a dark stone room. Dim figures in bloody labcoats linger in the shadows not too far away, shocked that he has stirred.
The cloaked figure from the Princess's tower turns towards him from his desk in front of Link, raises a sinister sword magically in the air, and flings it at Link-

Link awakens at the bottom of a dark pool.
He struggles. He wrestles with the waters around him.
He runs out of strength.
The Triforce of Courage awakens in his hand.
He wrestles and writhes- He breaks free! Invisible hands turn visible (pale, bony things) as they reach after him and he swims to the surface beyond their reach!
He awakens on dry ground, no water in sight. The world seems to be under a veil of twilight, but for the lack of floating black squares.
He is in front of the Forest Temple.

[Arc 2: The Fused Shadow Dungeons]
<Part 1>
There is the bird that sells red potion and lantern oil. It acts normal, but it is of the holographic texture of Phantom Zant. (Link has nothing still, not even an empty bottle.)
(Hearts do not drop in this game, nor are there fairies to obtain. All healing must be obtained from other sources, such as the holographic merchants.)
There's nowhere to go... but into the dungeon.
Still under squareless twilight, the Forest Temple is creepier than before. Poison mist blankets floors. Water is often toxic too. (Neither compass nor map are ever given in this game.)
Though many of the usual monsters appear, there are also foes that would normally be in Arbiter's Grounds: stalfos and redead rising out of tall grass, bubbles patrolling walkways.
The first items obtained are an empty bottle and a lantern. With these, Link can exit the temple and traverse the poison mist of Faron Woods. Out there he obtains the Ordon Sword.
(All hidden skills are already known.)
Within the dungeon again, Link makes his way to the midboss: a holographic version of Ook the monkey. (All the monkeys are holographic.) From him Link gets the Gale Boomerang.
Link reaches the boss: a harder version of Dia Baba. It constantly attacks.
Ook never appears. The bombling logs are rolled against thte back wall, requiring Link to turn to target them, and to be wary of getting too close.
Upon defeat, the boss explodes into a roaring cloud of ash. It clears, revealing that Link has been teleported to the Goron's sumo room. (Link cannot return to the previous region.)
<Part 2>
The exit is blocked with a boulder, so Link cannot descend the mountain until he gets the iron boots, enabling use of the elevator to Death Mountain Hot Springs.
Link enters the Goron Mines. Inside, he finds some rooms dropping lava bombs from volcanic eruptions, and other rooms full of smoke/fog.
After obtaining the iron boots, Link returns outside. There is a holographic goron merchant at the hot springs. The waters retain their healing properties, something available for free.
Bring a bottle of hot spring water to the holographic goron at the foot of Death Mountain Trail to get him to break the boulder blocking access to Kakariko Village.
The town is closed at both ends. Eruptions might occur even here, and there are probably lots of bulblins.
Get the Inn key from the graveyard, and the Sanctuary key from the Inn, then get the bomb bag in the Sanctuary basement cannon room. (More bombs can be bought from Barnes.)
Opt: After getting the hero's bow from the mines, Link can find the hawkeye under a boulder on a ridge near the highest point in the village. (Use bomb-arrows and the boomerang.)
The wooden shield may be sold at the local Malo Mart.
Fyrus is also harder. Following the style of an OoT flaredancer, he changes colors per phase:
- Red: He leaves a trail of fire whenever he's blinded, making it harder to grab the chains safely.
- Blue: He hops up and down, dropping lava rocks on your location.
- Green: He dims and fills the room with smoke, making it harder to spot his forehead crystal.
Upon defeat, the ground shatters, dropping Link into the mostly drained Lake Hylia. (Link never gets new heart containers.)
<Part 3>
A holographic zora merchant is found outside Lakebed Temple.
Link has to traverse a long underwater area before he obtains the zora armor, nearly drowning in the process.
Other items of the dungeon/region include the clawshot and the water bombs. The region is porbably no bigger than Lake Hylia's upper caves (use the clawshot on peahats to reach).
Morpheel is harder... somehow.
Upon defeat, the wall Morpheel headbutts breaks open, sucking Link into a dark abyss with the draining water.
Link finds himself fighting a horde of crazy Illias from Lanayru's vision. A light eventually intervenes, sending him to the entrance of Arbiter's Grounds.
<Part 4>
Arbiter's Grounds requires the lantern in most every room, only shedding light where it would be necessary to escape if the oil runs out.
The first item is the shadow crystal that turns Link into a wolf (no Midna to handle it, though). He cannot learn scents in this game.
Exiting the temple, wolf Link enters a grotto on the opposite side of the desert where he finds the spinner. (No merchants in this region.)
Completing the dungeon, defeating Stallord properly, Link is not teleported but continues to the roof as in the original adventure.
There he finds the cloaked figure from before, the one who stabbed him.
Dream overlaps with reality: the desert rooftop blends with the dark dungeon he's still chained in- still stabbed.
The cloaked figure reveals his identity:
The fallen Sage of Water that Ganondorf killed.
Bongo Bongo.
<Part 5>


